<body>
  <span class="tag">throwaway prototype · branch angle-hypotheses</span>

  <h1>Round 2 — three interpretations of the derived spine</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Built after the reasoning rounds, from the converged law in <code>NOTES.md</code>:
    seduction before catch, containers cut from each work's own material, respond-never-perform,
    residue instead of verdicts, facts flat, looking counts as use. All three obey the law;
    they differ only on the site-level container. Real Metro records run in each.
  </p>

  <div class="grid" style="margin-bottom:3.5rem">
    <a class="card" href="/prototype/flow/">
      <span class="key">FLOW</span>
      <h2>Works forward</h2>
      <p class="pov">"Home is the works: one flow of self-shaped, unequal containers, flagship first."</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <p><strong>About/CV:</strong> flat section at the end.</p>
        <p><strong>Bet:</strong> a hurried visitor meets proof in five seconds.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/prototype/record/">
      <span class="key">RECORD</span>
      <h2>One whole-person record</h2>
      <p class="pov">"One flat list of everything since physics, ordered by last use. No About page."</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <p><strong>About/CV:</strong> entries in the same grammar as the works.</p>
        <p><strong>Bet:</strong> the no-separate-rooms claim made structural.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/prototype/merge/">
      <span class="key">MERGE</span>
      <h2>The flow is the record</h2>
      <p class="pov">"One record, last-used order, but each entry takes the shape its contents honestly support."</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <p><strong>About/CV:</strong> in the record as data lines and testimony.</p>
        <p><strong>Bet:</strong> both bets at once, priced by the ladder.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h1 style="font-size:1.3rem">Round 1 — the four original angle hypotheses (kept for reference)</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Same content pool arranged under four directorial points of view, before the reasoning rounds.
    Divya's verdict: A and D had something interesting; B and C were mix-and-match. The audits that
    followed are summarized in <code>NOTES.md</code>.
  </p>

  <div class="grid">
    <a class="card" href="/prototype/a/">
      <span class="key">A</span>
      <h2>No glass</h2>
      <p class="pov">"The site is the first piece. You're already inside it."</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <p><strong>Highlights:</strong> the experiences themselves — everything is a door with a verb: enter, play, touch, look.</p>
        <p><strong>Demotes:</strong> biography, credentials, chronology.</p>
        <p><strong>Speaks to:</strong> audiences, juries, anyone who should <em>feel</em> the claim instead of reading it.</p>
        <p><strong>Risk:</strong> a recruiter or curator in a hurry finds no resume surface.</p>
      </div>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="/prototype/b/">
      <span class="key">B</span>
      <h2>Ships builds</h2>
      <p class="pov">"A maker who builds interactive things end to end. The proof is the list."</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <p><strong>Highlights:</strong> capability and cadence — dated rows, live links, stacks, self-built tools.</p>
        <p><strong>Demotes:</strong> mood, atmosphere, long emotional copy.</p>
        <p><strong>Speaks to:</strong> hirers, collaborators, fellowship panels who count shipped things.</p>
        <p><strong>Risk:</strong> the feeling-first half of the practice reads as a footnote.</p>
      </div>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="/prototype/c/">
      <span class="key">C</span>
      <h2>Look again</h2>
      <p class="pov">"One inquiry, many forms: what gets ignored, and what participation does to looking."</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <p><strong>Highlights:</strong> coherence — projects as chapters of one question; illustration fused in, not shelved.</p>
        <p><strong>Demotes:</strong> stack-flexing, listings, anything catalogue-like.</p>
        <p><strong>Speaks to:</strong> residency juries, curators, research fellowships.</p>
        <p><strong>Risk:</strong> slower to read; "interactive" arrives late.</p>
      </div>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="/prototype/d/">
      <span class="key">D</span>
      <h2>Worlds, with people</h2>
      <p class="pov">"Interactive worlds you can step into — each with its own feeling, most made with people she loves."</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <p><strong>Highlights:</strong> emotional range, narrative craft, collaborators credited on the work itself.</p>
        <p><strong>Demotes:</strong> tech stacks, credentials, dates.</p>
        <p><strong>Speaks to:</strong> narrative/game people, studios, kindred makers.</p>
        <p><strong>Risk:</strong> least "engineer" signal of the four.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    These are layout hypotheses, not designs — the winner (or the parts you steal from each) becomes
    the brief for the real restructure. The whole <code>src/pages/prototype/</code> folder gets deleted after.
  </p>
</body>
</html>
